erage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_075","cnm_148","cnm_080"],"id":"product-and-completing-the-square","label":"Use the product, then complete the square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"product-and-completing-the-square":["cnm_226","cnm_221","cnm_220","cnm_077"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_226","cnm_221","cnm_220","cnm_077","cnm_075","cnm_148","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C","reason":"Irrational numbers have nonterminating, nonrepeating decimal expansions. The examples 0.333…, √4=2, and √2+(-√2)=0 refute A, B, and D, respectively.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_077_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_075","cnm_080"],"id":"cnm_077_q4_m1","label":"Definition and counterexamples","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_077_q4_m1":["cnm_077","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_077","cnm_071","cnm_075","cnm_080"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: an infinite non-repeating decimal","reason":"If √2 were a reduced fraction p/q, then p²=2q² would force both p and q to be even. Thus √2 is irrational and its decimal expansion neither terminates nor repeats.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q33","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_003","cnm_p168","cnm_074"],"id":"irrationality-by-parity","label":"Show that no reduced fraction is possible","methodDirectNodeIds":{"irrationality-by-parity":["cnm_077","cnm_p156"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_077","cnm_p156","cnm_003","cnm_p168","cnm_074"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (20; a = -13, b = 33)","reason":"Substituting 4+√5 gives (19+4a+b)+(13+a)√5 = 0. Since a and b are integers and √5 is irrational, a = -13 and b = 33, so a+b = 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_077"],"id":"compare-rational-and-irrational-parts","label":"Substitute and separate irrational parts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-rational-and-irrational-parts":["cnm_173","math:rational-irrational-operation-proofs"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_173","cnm_077","math:rational-irrational-operation-proofs"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"4","reason":"The rational numbers are -2, 0, 1/2, and -√9=-3, giving four.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_078_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_075"],"id":"cnm_078_q2_m1","label":"Simplify and classify","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_078_q2_m1":["cnm_078","cnm_077"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_078","cnm_077","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 7","reason":"For two integer points on the circle, d² = 50 - 2(x₁x₂ + y₁y₂), so d² is a positive even integer at most 100. Irrationality excludes d² = 100, giving √2 ≤ d ≤ √98. Points P=(3,4), Q=(-4,-3), R=(3,-4), S=(4,-3) give PQ=√98 and RS=√2, attaining ratio 7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_10A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"antipodal-chord-bounds","label":"Bound chords using opposite points","methodDirectNodeIds":{"antipodal-chord-bounds":["math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_249","cnm_077"]},"nodeIds":["math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_249","cnm_077"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (6 entries)","reason":"For both rational-slope rows, only zero or more than two lattice points are possible: one lattice point generates infinitely many. For irrational slope, only zero or exactly one is possible, since two would force rational slope. Both occur: for irrational m, y = m(x + 1/2) has none and y = mx has only the origin. Thus 2 + 2 + 2 = 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_003","cnm_077","cnm_082"],"id":"classify-by-slope","label":"Construct points and restrict the slope","methodDirectNodeIds":{"classify-by-slope":["cnm_h167","cnm_h169"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h167","cnm_h169","cnm_003","cnm_077","cnm_082"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E","reason":"For bases u,v and altitude h, u+v=2h, so K=(u+v)h/2=h². Positive choices of h can make K an integer, a noninteger rational number, or an irrational number, so none of A through D is forced.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_003","cnm_077","cnm_074","cnm_p105"],"id":"derive-area-and-realize-number-types","label":"Find the area formula and test what it forces","methodDirectNodeIds":{"derive-area-and-realize-number-types":["cnm_h203","cnm_p149","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h203","cnm_p149","cnm_071","cnm_003","cnm_077","cnm_074","cnm_p105"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (5/9)","reason":"Projections, or the cosine law with ∠ADE=135°, give AE²=s²+r²+√2sr. Hence AF²=s²+√2sr=9+5√2. Since r and s are rational and √2 is irrational, s²=9 and sr=5, so r/s=5/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_077","cnm_260","cnm_075"],"id":"diagonals-and-irrationality","label":"Use the square's diagonals","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonals-and-irrationality":["cnm_191","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_262","cnm_174","cnm_173","cnm_077","cnm_260","cnm_075"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 127; s = √(85 + 42√2)","reason":"Set C = (0,0), A = (s,0), B = (0,s), P = (x,y), and t = s². The distances give x² + y² = 36, 2sx = t - 85, and 2sy = t - 13, hence t² - 170t + 3697 = 0. Thus t = 85 ± 42√2; interiority gives x > 0 and t > 85, so a + b = 85 + 42 = 127.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"],"id":"solution:coordinate-distances:9bdb663b9601","label":"Subtract coordinate distance equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-distances":["cnm_h173","cnm_174","cnm_221"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h173","cnm_174","cnm_221","cnm_082","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_245","cnm_115","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"],"id":"solution:quarter-turn-construction:9bdb663b9601","label":"Rotate to construct a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quarter-turn-construction":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_247","cnm_177","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_174","cnm_245","cnm_115","cnm_148","cnm_171","cnm_077"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: only the fourth number is rational","reason":"The first number is pi. Neither 4/5 is a rational cube nor 0.00016=1/6250 a rational fourth power, as their prime exponents show. The fourth number is -sqrt(100/9)=-10/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1958_AMC_12_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_003","cnm_077","cnm_074","cnm_p168"],"id":"classify-four-numbers","label":"Evaluate exact roots and test the remaining roots"